{"route": "cv_question", "lang": "en", "answer": "Christopher is a DevOps/Platform Engineer at DATEV eG in Nürnberg, where he has worked since 2019, first as an apprentice and since 2022 in his current role. He works in a team that runs Apache Kafka and RabbitMQ as a managed service for more than 100 development teams, with a 99.99 % SLA. Kafka runs on six clusters across two private data centers, and RabbitMQ runs on Kubernetes with quorum queues.

He builds self-service and automation for these platforms. He develops Cloud Foundry service brokers in Java (Spring Boot) that let teams provision Kafka resources themselves, and he helped build GitOps-based automation for RabbitMQ clusters. He also works with Terraform, Kubernetes, Crossplane, Prometheus and Grafana, HashiCorp Vault, and the Elastic Stack.

Christopher uses agentic development with GitHub Copilot and co-leads a team-internal Shared Knowledge Hub for AI coding agents, which is used in 10 projects. He is also building a local LLM proof of concept for Kafka operations and a multi-agent system in a private project.", "tool_calls": [], "show_form": false}

{"route": "cv_question", "lang": "en", "answer": "Yes, both. The MCP and RAG work is a real project, not just a course.

At DATEV, Christopher co-leads the Shared Knowledge Hub since 07/2026, together with a colleague. It is a shared context store for AI coding agents, and agents reach it through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). It uses hybrid search, combining keyword search and semantic search over embeddings. The hub is used in 10 projects today, and a rollout to about 30 projects is planned.

He has also completed Claude Academy courses on MCP, including "Introduction to Model Context Protocol" and "Model Context Protocol: Advanced topics", both issued 11/2026. Those are online course certificates. His hands-on MCP work is in the hub and in his private AgentBrain project and LLM-Ops proof of concept, where a local Kafka agent uses MCP tools.", "tool_calls": [], "show_form": false}

